#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a326dcea-688c-5509-8fb9-601576303172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a326dcea-688c-5509-8fb9-601576303172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275379} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c2a923-d5e7-5590-bc52-0f6cc0047f55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6b9bd3-a818-5c1f-8180-a6bf6005034c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338740" :question/problem "The solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3=0$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\cdot {360}^{∘}={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={360}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff0a002-3973-5a9a-a567-7a70167408af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:answer/id #uuid "0d519fae-e17c-5d7d-8f7a-f980143ef52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eb3064-23fa-5a31-9d11-1d0a7cfe0056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebde0d0-de97-5a39-aa75-9519a44f1c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:answer/id #uuid "79958a5f-7948-5d86-be84-ea6cd92d5246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79958a5f-7948-5d86-be84-ea6cd92d5246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} :answer-field/id #uuid "550d436d-d594-500b-ada4-d1a6aaca249a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02ea7373-ec80-585d-91db-f4a1e9445395" :question/math-academy-id "q-72838" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{4}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})=0.962\\,550…≈0.962\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.962\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.962\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/b2cd7e76526632d8507ba484246a560829882e7f10d9814f01593f7a48d9a2ac.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.962\\,6 \\\\  & =5.320\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈0.962\\,6\\cdot 5.320\\,6≈5.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7b2ae3-796b-50d4-9728-2f5ce2ea8284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{45}^{∘},\\,{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275389} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2d5cba-808b-5a3f-a350-a13efd6c08d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{60}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c82973-2be4-57d6-ab3d-68cd07239945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{60}^{∘},\\,{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd6cdc7-0eff-56a5-9aaa-52e86028372a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275392} {:answer/id #uuid "82757984-e31e-5194-a518-369ddfad36de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82757984-e31e-5194-a518-369ddfad36de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9dbf31-2460-5f6f-b52b-38ed0958839b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a89d97f-778a-52c7-aa22-e2fa1943ed51" :question/math-academy-id "q-72842" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}=-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7d3649c-ea73-5e11-9842-12a6f85fda82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396} {:answer/id #uuid "1291be03-654b-5964-a173-9590e65948f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397} {:answer/id #uuid "553ccdfe-be40-5ff8-a802-d063b23c76d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398} {:answer/id #uuid "2071ca9c-953d-5669-bae2-78765b56b9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399} {:answer/id #uuid "76e51f90-4214-51f5-810f-eea233ea56c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "553ccdfe-be40-5ff8-a802-d063b23c76d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398} :answer-field/id #uuid "08c4c571-7c9f-5f0d-8907-2215d3fb1415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72db97ad-a1bd-57cd-939e-1ca6b4662765" :question/math-academy-id "q-8683" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/caa6662d8349751e35bcb1af4196ad043b66ea64f21805eb6ca9b14435d38458.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33ee2e6-acdb-565c-b9e0-f8118cbc46be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275403} {:answer/id #uuid "68778839-6e8c-58b1-869e-5fee45b398d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275404} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8e93dd-b7c6-5c75-9bb1-5bc74760c82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275405} {:answer/id #uuid "71ddca9f-d8f0-5e98-b33a-fe8a2c753fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275406} {:answer/id #uuid "274bcc2e-5993-56f8-85e6-7ab6d6bef419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad8e93dd-b7c6-5c75-9bb1-5bc74760c82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275405} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0f7a725-0df3-5ef2-b76c-340293306dc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f86c6bc4-9f8a-51c8-96fd-8b033c2a999c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8690" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=1.738\\,244…≈1.738\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.738$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.738.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.738 & ≈1.404.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.404.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/392ae25b9a2aa47e5292d7e623922803891b52099d20525f888daca92642ce88.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.404 \\\\  & =4.546\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.546-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-1.738\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.738\\cdot (-1.738)≈-3\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
